    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Figure 1. An example for K4."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 28,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.72, 420.903, 485.41, 491.312 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "from v1. Initially we may let P follow either e14 or e12. Choose to follow e14. Keep extending P in this way, we may get P = e14e43e31e12e24e43e31. At this stage P is a circuit and the “while” loop is exited, and we have to translate P to ﬁnd a “non-saturated” vertex. The ﬁrst such a vertex we get is v4. By translation, now P becomes e43e31e12e24e43e31e14. Extending P, we will ﬁnally get a directionconsistent circuit C = e43e31e12e24e43e31e14e43e32e24 such that Cab = α."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 29,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.721, 356.189, 533.881, 413.911 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "3.2. Counting direction-consistent circuits: generalized BEST theorem. The well-known BEST theorem, named after de Bruijn and van Aardenne-Ehrenfest [vAEdB51] and Smith and Tutte [TS41], provides an eﬃcient algorithm of counting Eulerian cycles on an Eulerian digraph Go (meaning that the in-degree and out-degree at each vertex of Go are identical)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 30,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.721, 271.229, 485.53, 354.152 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Recall that an arborescence T of Go with a root vertex w is a spanning tree of G such that all edges on T are oriented towards w with respect to o. Recall also that the Laplacian matrix of Go, denoted L(Go), is deﬁned as: (i) for i = j, (L(Go))ij is the negation of the number of positively oriented edges with respect to o with initial vertex vi and terminal vertex vj, and (ii) (L(Go))ii = − j =i(L(Go))ij. Removing the row and column of L(Go) with respect to w, we get the reduced Laplacian Lred(w)(Go) at w."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 31,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.72, 197.909, 485.4, 269.312 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Denote by ec(G,o) the number of Eulerian cycles on Go, and Inw(G,o) the number of arborescences Go rooted at w. Then the BEST theorem states that ec(G,o) = Inw(G,o)· v∈V(G)(deg+o (v)−1)! where deg+o (v) is the out-degree of Go at v ∈ V (G). Note that by a directed version of Kirchhoﬀ’s matrix-tree theorem, Inw(G,o) is actually independent of w, and can be eﬃciently computed as the determinant of the reduced Laplacian matrix Lred(w)(Go)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 32,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.721, 142.256, 485.411, 195.871 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Before making a generalization of the BEST theorem, we ﬁrst introduce a notion of weighted Laplacian here. Deﬁnition 3.5. Consider a graph G. Let α ∈ C1(G,Z) be universal. Let ce = degα(e) for all e ∈ E(G). Then α = e∈E"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 33,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.721, 116.216, 485.342, 154.691 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "o(α)(G) ce · e with all ce > 0. The weighted Laplacian Lα(G) of G with respect to α is deﬁned as: (Lα(G))ij = − e∈E"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 35,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.72, 91.507, 512.69, 128.432 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "o(α)(G),e(0)=vi,e(1) =vi ce for all diagonal entries. Removing the row and column of Lα with respect to w, we get the reduced weighted Laplacian Lred(α w)(G)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 34,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 162.001, 115.394, 415.327, 128.432 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "o(α)(G),e(0)=vi,e(1)=vj ce for all i = j, and (Lα(G))ii = e∈E"
  } ]
